Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can wire everything together.  
It assumes that the object returned by `functions.B` behaves like a classic “async‑style” queue – i.e. it exposes an `.on()` method for listening to events and a `.push()` (or `.add()`) method for queuing work items.  

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the queue – concurrency = 5
const queue = functions.B({
  t: '5',          // <-- concurrency level (string form)
  // e and n are optional – we’ll add tasks later
});

// 2️⃣ Attach the two event listeners you asked for
queue.on('saturated',   () => console.log('Queue is saturated'));
queue.on('unsaturated', () => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));

// 3️⃣ Helper that creates a tiny “task” which simply logs a message.
//    The task itself is a function that will be executed by the queue.
const makeTask = (msg) => () => {
  console.log(msg);
};

// 4️⃣ Push a bunch of tasks onto the queue.
//    Because we’re pushing them all at once, the queue will hit its
//    concurrency limit (5) very quickly – triggering the “saturated”
//    event. As soon as any of those tasks finish, the “unsaturated”
//    event will fire again.
for (let i = 1; i <= 12; i++) {
  queue.push(makeTask(`Task #${i}`));
}
```

### What’s happening under the hood?

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Create** | `functions.B({t:'5'})` returns a queue instance. | The `t` argument tells the queue to allow *five* workers to run concurrently. |
| **Listen** | `queue.on('saturated', …)` & `queue.on('unsaturated', …)` | These are standard event hooks that fire automatically when the queue’s internal state changes. |
| **Add tasks** | `queue.push(makeTask(...))` | Each call queues a new job. Because we’re queuing *more* jobs than the concurrency limit, the queue will reach saturation almost instantly. |

> **Note:**  
> - The exact method names (`push`, `add`, etc.) depend on the implementation of `functions.B`.  
> - If your queue expects a callback (as many async libraries do), you can modify `makeTask` to accept a `done` argument and invoke it after logging.  
> - If you want to see the saturation effect more clearly, you can replace the body of `makeTask` with a small `setTimeout` so that each job takes a fraction of a second to complete.

That’s all you need to get a queue that logs “Queue is saturated” when it hits five concurrent workers and “Queue is unsaturated” when it drops below that threshold.

